{"name":"Hidden Cottage","hex":"#8d7f64","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hidden","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["past participle of hide"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Located or positioned out of sight; not visually apparent.","Obscure. (Can we add an example for this sense?)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hidden"},{"term":"cottage","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small house.","A seasonal home of any size or stature, a recreational home or a home in a remote location."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To stay at a seasonal home, to go cottaging.","To have homosexual sex in a public lavatory; to practice cottaging."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cottage"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cottage","blocks":[{"text":"A cottage during England's feudal period was the holding by a cottager of a small house with enough garden to feed a family and in return for the cottage, the cottager had to provide some form of service to the manorial lord. However, in time cottage just became the general term for a small house. In modern usage, a cottage is usually a modest, often cosy dwelling, typically in a rural or semi-rural location and not necessarily in England. The cottage orné, often quite large and grand residences built by the nobility, dates back to a movement of \"rustic\" stylised cottages of the late 18th and early 19th century during the Romantic movement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Country_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Country_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cottage","wikidata":"Q5783996"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/South_Devon_cottage_-_geograph.org.uk_-_81799.jpg/500px-South_Devon_cottage_-_geograp